\include scripts/document.script

\def headline {\italic{A Basic Document}\hfil The document format}
\def footline {\hfil — \the\pageno —\hfil}

\title {A Basic Document Format}
\author {Edward A. Maxedon}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

\section {Introduction}

This document exercises the basic format provided by \emph{document.script}. It
shows the title block above, automatically numbered sections, ordinary justified
paragraphs, emphasis, quotations, and both kinds of list. The body is set in
Latin Modern Roman, the default serif face.

A second paragraph follows to show the normal first-line indent that separates
one paragraph from the next. There is no extra vertical space between body
paragraphs; the indent alone marks the boundary, in the traditional book style.

\subsection {Emphasis}

Words can be set in \emph{italic} for emphasis, and whole phrases such as
\emph{this clause set apart from the rest} are handled the same way.

\subsection {Quotations}

A quotation block is indented on both sides and remains fully justified:

\begin{quote}
The art of writing is the art of discovering what you believe. Good prose is
like a windowpane, clear enough that the reader forgets it is there at all and
sees only what lies beyond it.
\end{quote}

The text returns to the full measure after the block closes.

\section {Lists}

Bulleted and numbered lists are available, and they nest.

\subsection {Bulleted lists}

\begin{itemize}
\item The first item is short.

\item The second item is deliberately long so that it wraps onto more than one
line, which lets you see that the continuation lines align neatly under the text
rather than under the bullet marker.

\item A third item, with a nested list inside it:

\begin{itemize}
\item A nested bullet.

\item Another nested bullet, also long enough to wrap so that the deeper hanging
indent is visible against the outer level.
\end{itemize}

\item Back out at the first level for the final item.
\end{itemize}

\subsection {Numbered lists}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Numbered items work the same way, with an arabic numeral and a period in
place of the bullet.

\item The numbering is automatic and restarts at one for each new list.

\item A final numbered item to round out the example.
\end{enumerate}

\vfill\eject
